var r={clip_points_to_area:"<h2>Keep points inside the area</h2><p><strong>A spatial operation.</strong> It takes a layer of points and a study area, and passes on the points that are in the area.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>Every point is tested against the study area's outline, and the ones inside come out the other side. The ones outside are counted and reported, not passed on.</p><p>The test is the same one the coverage check and the map use. A point counted inside here is inside there, and a figure you quote from one agrees with the other.</p><h3>Why you need it</h3><p>A source gives you what its own request asked for, and almost every source is bounded by a <strong>rectangle</strong>: OpenStreetMap's query takes a bounding box, a cloud dataset is read by extent, a country export is clipped to one. A rectangle is not a jurisdiction. Draw North Kivu \u2014 long, curved, a province of the DRC \u2014 and its bounding box covers large parts of Uganda and Rwanda; a facility search inside it returned 961 places of which 526 stood in the neighbouring countries.</p><p>This widget is where that is decided, visibly, on the canvas. Before it existed the decision was made privately inside whichever widget happened to need it, which meant it could not be seen, changed, or recorded.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. Connect the study area to the <strong>area</strong> port and the point layer to the <strong>points</strong> port. Both are required: the question is about these points against that area. 2. Leave <strong>Keep points that sit exactly on the outline</strong> ticked unless you need strict containment. 3. Run. The step reports how many were inside, how many sat on the boundary and how many fell outside, and passes the kept points on to whatever you wire next.</p><p>If every point falls outside, the step refuses and says so with the counts \u2014 that usually means the area is not the one you meant, or the points are somewhere else entirely.</p><h3>The one setting</h3><p><strong>Keep points that sit exactly on the outline.</strong> On by default. A point on the border is in that jurisdiction's edge, and silently folding it into one side or the other answers a question nobody asked. Turn it off for strict containment \u2014 which is what the OGC predicate <code>sfWithin</code> means, and it gives a different count on exactly the data where it matters.</p><h3>Seeing what it set aside</h3><p><strong>Show the excluded points on a connected map.</strong> On by default. They are drawn in the colour the map already uses for points outside the area, and they are <strong>not</strong> passed to anything downstream \u2014 the next widget receives only what is in the area.</p><p>The count tells you how many were set aside. Seeing them tells you whether setting them aside was right: 526 facilities in a tight cluster over one border is a different fact from 526 scattered in every direction, and no number distinguishes those.</p><h3>What it records</h3><p>The receipt names the predicate the kept set answers to, whether boundary points were kept, and three counts: inside, on the boundary, outside. A reader can tell how many the source offered and how many this area accepted, which is the number a reviewer asks for.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><p>It does not change a point. Nothing is moved, snapped or rounded; a point either is in the area or is not.</p><p>It does not know why a point is outside. A facility across a border, a coordinate entered the wrong way round and a geocoding error all look the same to a membership test. The coverage check is the widget for asking what to do about them, with reasons recorded.</p><p>It does not clip polygons or rasters. Those have their own widgets.</p><h3>Worked examples that use it</h3><p>None yet. It was built on 2026-10-20, from a defect found in a real study area.</p>",deduplicate_points:"<h2>Deduplicate point records</h2><p><strong>A comparison.</strong> It takes two or more point layers from different sources, finds the records that are the same place, and merges the pairs that agree. The pairs it is unsure about it gives to you.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>Two sources over one study area draw the same hospital twice. A count of facilities is then wrong by however many of those there are, and no amount of care downstream fixes it, because nothing downstream knows which records were the same place.</p><p>This compares records <strong>across</strong> sources and asks two questions of every pair it considers:</p><ul><li><strong>Do the names agree?</strong> Either exactly, after normalising, or by similarity.</li><li><strong>Are they in the same place?</strong> Within a distance you set.</li></ul><p>Each question that answers yes is worth one point. A pair scoring two merges on its own. A pair scoring one <strong>waits for you</strong>, because the two questions disagree and that is a judgement rather than a calculation. A pair scoring none was never a candidate.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><p>Two or more <strong>point</strong> layers, each from a different source. One layer has nothing to compare with, so two is the floor rather than the shape; use <strong>Add point input</strong> for a third and a fourth.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it decides</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Name attribute</strong></td><td>Which attribute carries the name. <code>name</code> for most sources.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Name comparison</strong></td><td>*Exact* after normalising, or *Jaro\u2013Winkler similarity*.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Similarity at or above</strong></td><td>The similarity at which two names count as agreeing.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Compare pairs within</strong></td><td>Pairs further apart than this are never compared at all.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Count as the same place within</strong></td><td>The distance at which two records are in one place.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Merge automatically at a score of</strong></td><td>Two means both questions must agree.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ask me at a score of</strong></td><td>One means a single agreement brings the pair to you.</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>Choosing the distances</h4><p>These are the two numbers worth measuring rather than accepting. Sources digitise a facility differently \u2014 one marks the building, another the compound gate, a third the signpost on the road \u2014 so how far apart two records of one facility sit is a property of <strong>your</strong> sources. Run it once with a generous *compare pairs within*, read the distances on the pairs it offers you, and set *count as the same place within* from what you see.</p><h4>Deciding a pair</h4><p>Select the widget and choose <strong>Review pairs</strong>. The dialog shows one pair at a time, with:</p><ul><li><strong>Both records side by side</strong>, each under the name of the source it came from.</li><li><strong>Every attribute either record carries</strong>, lined up in one table, with the fields that agree</li></ul><p>marked apart from the fields that differ. This is usually what settles it: a second field agreeing, or a second field flatly disagreeing. A field missing on one side shows as a gap, because a missing value is not agreement.</p><ul><li><strong>How far apart</strong> the records are, <strong>how closely the names matched</strong>, and which of the two tests</li></ul><p>agreed.</p><p>Answer <strong>The same place</strong> or <strong>Different places</strong>, and say why. The reason is required: a judgement nobody can read six months later is not a record of one. It is kept with the decision, and the decision is pinned to the records as they were \u2014 <strong>edit either record and the judgement lapses</strong>, rather than a stale answer riding on changed data. A decision can be taken back from the same dialog.</p><h3>Reading what you get</h3><p>A point layer with one record per place. A merged record keeps the first source's values and carries a <code>mergedFrom</code> attribute naming every record that went into it. Records with no usable location pass through untouched \u2014 they were never candidates, and nothing has been said about them.</p><p>The receipt records the whole rule: the name method and its threshold, both distances, both score thresholds, how many pairs were compared, how many the blocking rule withheld, how many matched, how many are still waiting, and for every candidate its score, its distance and its name similarity. A figure that reports a deduplicated count can therefore say what produced it.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It will not look inside one source.</strong> Comparison is across sources, so a layer's duplicates of</li></ul><p>itself are not found.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not read abbreviations.</strong> <code>HGR Goma</code> and <code>H\xF4pital G\xE9n\xE9ral de R\xE9f\xE9rence de Goma</code> are one</li></ul><p>facility and this will not say so: there is no nickname dictionary, no transliteration, no phonetic key and no learned model. That pair reaches you in the review band, which is where the judgement belongs.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not merge on a name alone</strong>, at the settings it ships with. Two clinics in one town can</li></ul><p>share a name; two unrelated facilities can stand twenty metres apart.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not decide for you.</strong> A pair in the review band stays two records until somebody</li></ul><p>answers. The gate is on the merge, not on the run.</p><ul><li><strong>It cannot tell you the thresholds are right.</strong> They are declarations about your data, and the</li></ul><p>only way to know is to look at the pairs it offers.</p><h3>Where the method comes from</h3><p>The contracts are those of <code>EPIAI RECORDLINK</code> \u2014 blocking before comparison, binary contributions rather than a blended score, three classes rather than merge-or-not, and a missing value that never agrees with another missing value. This widget adds the spatial comparison and the merge the thresholds licence.</p>",derive_value:"<h2>Derive a value</h2><p><strong>Arithmetic.</strong> It takes a table, combines two of its values with one operation, and gives the table back with a new value added for every group of rows.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>A table here is <strong>long</strong>: every row carries one value, under key columns. So two numbers about the same subject are two rows, not two columns. Snow's Table IX holds</p><table><thead><tr><th>supplier</th><th>measure</th><th>count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Southwark and Vauxhall Company</td><td>houses</td><td>40,046</td></tr><tr><td>Southwark and Vauxhall Company</td><td>deaths</td><td>1,263</td></tr></tbody></table><p>and the number a 2\xD72 needs \u2014 <strong>houses without a death</strong> \u2014 is in neither row. It is <code>houses</code> minus <code>deaths</code>, and until this widget existed the only way to get it was to compute it somewhere else and import the answer.</p><p>This step computes it once per group: all the rows sharing the other key columns are one group, and <code>measure</code> is the column holding the names of the values to combine.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><p><strong>records</strong> \u2014 a table. It gives back the same table with the derived rows added.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it decides</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Key column holding the values</strong></td><td>Which column names the values. In Snow's table, <code>measure</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>First value</strong></td><td>The left operand, e.g. <code>houses</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Operation</strong></td><td>minus, plus, times, divided by.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Second value</strong></td><td>The right operand, e.g. <code>deaths</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Name for the derived value</strong></td><td>What the new rows are called, e.g. <code>houses without a death</code>.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The values are offered as a list wherever the chain ends in a table this canvas holds, including through an earlier *Derive a value* or *Select rows by value*. Where it does not, you type them.</p><h3>What it refuses, and why</h3><p><strong>A group missing either value gets no row, and is named in the evidence.</strong> A supplier whose house count nobody recorded has an *unknown* number of houses without a death. Writing a zero there would be a measurement the table never made.</p><p><strong>A name the column already uses is refused.</strong> Two rows would then share a key, and a table with two values for one key is one nobody can read.</p><p><strong>Dividing by zero is unknown, not infinite.</strong></p><h3>What it will not do</h3><p>It will not tell you whether the two values can be combined. Subtracting deaths from houses works because both count houses; subtracting a rate from a count is equally allowed here and is nonsense. Check that yourself before reading the answer.</p><p>It will not fill in a group whose values are incomplete. Those groups come back named, with no derived row, and any figure you draw from the result is missing them \u2014 count them before you present it.</p><p>It will not reach across tables. Both values must already be in the one table you connect.</p><h3>What this does not establish</h3><p>The widget does not know what its numbers count. Subtracting deaths from houses is sound only because both count houses; subtracting a rate from a count is equally permitted here and is nonsense. Nothing checks that the two values are commensurable, because nothing in the table says so \u2014 the unit belongs to the table as a whole, not to each measure.</p><p>A derived value is also not a measurement. <code>houses without a death</code> was never observed; it is an inference from two things that were, and it inherits every error in both.</p>",group_table:"<h2>Total within groups</h2><p><strong>Addition.</strong> It takes a table, keeps the key columns you name, and adds up the values of every row that agrees on them.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>32 sub-districts, each with a population and a death count, each belonging to one of three water supply categories. Totalling within the supply category and the measure turns 64 rows into six: the people and the deaths in each category. That six-row table is a contingency table, and it is what the next steps turn into a two-by-two.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><p><strong>records</strong> \u2014 any table.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it decides</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Keep</strong></td><td>the key columns that survive. Rows agreeing on all of them become one row.</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>Why you name what is kept rather than what is dropped</h4><p>Because files change. Name the columns you keep and a column added to the file later is simply dropped, leaving you the same groups you had before. Name the columns you drop and that new column silently becomes a grouping key, splitting your groups without telling you.</p><h4>Why it only adds up</h4><p>Adding counts gives you a count. Adding <strong>rates</strong> gives you a number with no referent \u2014 deaths per 1,000 in three sub-districts do not add up to deaths per 3,000 in one. This step cannot tell a count from a rate, so it offers the operation that is right for counts and does not offer a choice it has no way to police. Averages are a different operation and are not built.</p><h3>Reading what you get</h3><p>One row per group, and a receipt stating which columns were kept, which were dropped, how many rows collapsed into how many groups, and every group that held an empty cell.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It will not treat an empty cell as a zero.</strong> A group holding any empty value totals to</li></ul><p><strong>nothing known</strong>, and is named in the receipt. This is the whole risk of the operation: summing over an empty cell gives a total that is too small by an amount nobody can state, and a stratum whose population was never recorded should come out *unknown* rather than *small*.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not check that the rows belong together.</strong> You named the columns; it adds up what</li></ul><p>agrees on them. If you ask it to total a column of rates it will do that, and the number will mean nothing.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not group by everything.</strong> That is a no-op, and it says so rather than handing back</li></ul><p>your table unchanged.</p><h3>Where it is used</h3><p>The South London example: *Died or survived, by water supply*.</p><h3>How it was checked</h3><p>Validation Lab check <strong>29</strong>, <code>29-group-table-vs-pandas.ipynb</code>, against a pandas groupby. All six totals of the Snow contingency table agree exactly \u2014 they are integer counts, so the tolerance is zero rather than small.</p><p>The empty cell is the case worth checking, and <strong>pandas has no option for the rule this widget states</strong>. <code>min_count=1</code> reads like it and is the first thing anyone writes: it bounds how many values must be *present*, not whether any are *absent*, so on eleven numbers and one empty cell it returns the eleven. On this data that is 147,945 people instead of nothing known \u2014 short by the one sub-district nobody recorded. The comparison that does hold is a per-group <code>sum(skipna=False)</code>.</p><p>What no check can verify is the protection that matters most here: summing is the only operation offered, because this step cannot tell a count from a rate.</p>",isochrone:`<h2>Network isochrone</h2><p><strong>A process widget.</strong> It draws the area you can reach on foot within a stated time, walking along real streets, from one or more sites.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>From each site it walks outward along the street network until the time runs out, then draws a band around every street it reached. The result is one region per site \u2014 not a circle, because streets are not straight and do not go everywhere.</p><p>A walk has three parts and this charges for all three: <strong>to</strong> the nearest junction, <strong>along</strong> the streets, and <strong>off</strong> the street at the end to reach a door.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Connect</th><th>What it needs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>your sites</td><td>the points to walk from \u2014 pumps, clinics, depots</td></tr><tr><td>a street network</td><td>from the <strong>Street network</strong> widget</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Out come polygons, one per site. Draw them on a Map, or count your records into them with <strong>Summarize points in polygons</strong>.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. <strong>Add it</strong> from the palette, under Catchments. 2. <strong>Connect your sites and a street network.</strong> 3. <strong>Set the budget.</strong> *Minutes* \xD7 *metres per minute* is how far someone can walk \u2014 5 minutes at 75 m/min is 375 m of street. 4. <strong>Set the final walk off the street</strong> \u2014 how far from a street someone can get to a door. The band is this wide where there is time left and narrower where there is not. 5. <strong>Set the snap limit</strong> \u2014 how far a site may be from the nearest street before the result says so. 6. <strong>Check the zone.</strong> Measuring happens in metres, so the UTM zone is part of the answer, and every measuring step in one workflow must agree on it. The run is refused by name if they disagree. 7. <strong>Run the workflow</strong>, then read the regions on a map and the alert in the Inspector.</p><h3>How the travel time is spent</h3><p>Shortest paths are Dijkstra over the network's directed edges and the metre lengths it supplies, including the partial length of a curved edge where the budget runs out part-way along it.</p><p>All three legs of the journey are charged against the same budget:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Leg</th><th>What it costs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>the straight walk from the site onto the network</td><td>distance to the snapped street, at walking speed</td></tr><tr><td>the streets</td><td>the shortest path, by directed edge and supplied length</td></tr><tr><td>the final walk off the street to a door</td><td>the band width where time allows it, narrower where it does not</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Charging the last leg rather than adding it is what stops a region overshooting the time it is drawn for. The off-street band is an approximation of coverage beside the streets, and holes inside it remain unless infill is enabled.</p><p>Two cases are marked rather than dropped, and the widget names both:</p><ul><li><strong>A site further from a street than the snap limit</strong> is routed anyway and marked off-network.</li><li><strong>A site whose walk onto the network alone spends the budget</strong> gets no catchment at all.</li></ul><p>Coverage can also be cut short by the graph itself: a region stops where the downloaded network stops, which is a property of the extent that was downloaded and not of the travel time.</p><h3>Worked examples that use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Example</th><th>What it does there</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>John Snow \xB7 walking catchments</strong></td><td>five-minute walking regions around the eight Soho pumps, drawn as a plot and as an interactive map, with the 1854 deaths counted into them</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It is not a service area or a routed travel time.</strong> It is an approximation of shape, drawn</li></ul><p>to be read. Nobody agreed to it and no router produced it.</p><ul><li><strong>It assumes the ground beside a street can be walked</strong>, at walking speed, in any direction.</li></ul><p>Near the edge of the budget that is where the region is most generous.</p><ul><li><strong>It does not drop a site that is far from the streets.</strong> Such a site is routed anyway, with</li></ul><p>the straight line to the nearest junction charged against its budget. Its region is marked, the widget glows red, and the Inspector names the leg. That straight line assumes you can cross whatever lies between, so the region reaches further than the ground allows.</p><ul><li><strong>A site whose walk to the street would spend the whole budget gets no region at all.</strong> It</li></ul><p>reaches nothing in the stated time, which is the answer. It is named in the alert, and the note on the result says the counts are over the sites that reached something.</p><h4>The direction it misleads</h4><p>All of the above push the same way: <strong>a poorly connected place looks better served than it is.</strong> Streets and footpaths are mapped unevenly, and informal settlements least of all. Read a region over sparsely mapped ground as optimistic, check the alert for sites routed off the network, and check how far each site had to go to reach a street.</p><h3>Checking it yourself</h3><p>The lab's <a href="https://digital-twin-ph.github.io/validation-lab/lab/index.html?path=19-snap-vs-pyproj.ipynb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">snapping notebook</a> measures the displacement on the example data, and the corridor check rebuilds the region from the stated rule with Shapely.</p>`,join_table_to_area:"<h2>Join a table to the study area</h2><p><strong>A join.</strong> It takes a table of values and a study area whose parts are named, matches each row to the part it names, and hands on polygons that carry the value. A map downstream can then shade them.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>A rate per sub-district and a boundary of sub-districts are two separate things until something puts one onto the other. Without that, the only map you can draw from a table of measures is a map of where the rows are, which for most data is a map of where people live.</p><p>What is being joined is <strong>a table and another table with geometry</strong>. A GeoJSON feature, a shapefile row and a GeoDataFrame row are each a row with a geometry column, so a boundary file carries attributes exactly as a CSV does. This widget matches the two on one named column.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><ul><li><strong>records</strong> \u2014 a table. Any widget that emits one will do: an imported table, a rate, a case</li></ul><p>series. The column you match on must be one of its key columns.</p><ul><li><strong>area</strong> \u2014 a study area whose parts carry names. An area drawn by hand has no named parts, so</li></ul><p>there is nothing to key on and this says so rather than drawing an empty choropleth that looks like a finding.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it decides</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Column naming each part</strong></td><td>Which of the table's key columns holds the part name. Offered from the connected table, not typed.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Match names</strong></td><td>*Ignoring punctuation and spacing*, or *Exactly as written*.</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>Choosing how names are matched</h4><p>Names are compared for <strong>equality</strong>, never for resemblance. There is no similarity score and no threshold to set, because a name either is the name of that part or it is not.</p><p>Ignoring punctuation and spacing is what matches <code>St. George, Camberwell</code> to <code>St. George  Camberwell</code>. On Snow's South London files it is the difference between <strong>23 of 32</strong> rows matching and <strong>32 of 32</strong>: the nine that fail differ only by a comma. It will <strong>not</strong> match an abbreviation to a full name, or a historical spelling to a modern one, and it says so rather than guessing.</p><p>If your files differ by more than punctuation, this widget is the wrong tool and will tell you which rows it could not place. Fix the names at the source, where the correction is a fact about the data rather than a setting on a node.</p><h3>Reading what you get</h3><p>Polygons, one feature per part, each carrying the matched row's value and the rest of its columns. The parts are kept separate rather than dissolved.</p><ul><li><strong>A part no row names</strong> is drawn and left empty, in a neutral shade \u2014 never in the lowest class.</li></ul><p>An unmatched boundary drawn pale reads as a finding of low incidence, which is the opposite of what it means.</p><ul><li><strong>Two parts sharing a name</strong> both take the value. That is true of a rate and is <strong>not</strong> true of a</li></ul><p>count: Streatham is two polygons, and both carry its rate, which is why Snow's 32 sub-districts draw as 33 parts.</p><p>The receipt records the column matched on, the normalisation used, how many rows matched, how many did not, and every part left without a value, so a figure made from this can say what produced it.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It will not guess at a name.</strong> No similarity, no threshold, no review band. A row it cannot</li></ul><p>place is reported, not approximated.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not aggregate.</strong> Two rows naming the same part are a duplicate-key problem in the</li></ul><p>table, and are reported as one rather than quietly summed or averaged.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not decide whether the number is worth mapping.</strong> Whether the rate was computed over</li></ul><p>the right denominator, whether the classes are honest, and whether a reader will take area for magnitude are questions this widget does not answer.</p><h3>How it was checked</h3><p>Validation Lab check <strong>31</strong>, <code>31-area-join-vs-pandas.ipynb</code>, against a pandas left merge. Every part takes the value a merge gives it, nulls included, and the counts agree \u2014 33 of 33 parts and 32 of 32 rows after folding, with Streatham's two parts carrying one value between them.</p><p><strong>What the folding bought, measured.</strong> On the raw strings only 23 of 32 rows match. The nine that need folding are all a comma: the table writes <code>St. George, Camberwell</code> where the boundary file writes <code>St. George  Camberwell</code>. A join on raw keys would have drawn a choropleth missing 28% of its sub-districts. The check also confirms the folding touched nothing *but* punctuation \u2014 it is not reimplemented there, because a second copy by the same author is not a second opinion, so what is checked is the property it must satisfy.</p><p><strong>And the data cannot supply a negative control</strong>, which is worth knowing when you rely on this. Snow's table and these boundaries contain no pair differing by more than punctuation, so nothing in the example tests a name that *ought* not to match \u2014 32 of 32 is also what a rule that matched too readily would score. The check constructs one: a part renamed to <code>Camberwell Green</code>, which this join reports unmatched. Jaro-Winkler to <code>Camberwell</code> is 0.925, so a similarity-based linkage at the usual 0.90 threshold would have accepted it. That figure is what having no threshold here is worth.</p>",map_output:"<h2>Map</h2><p><strong>An output widget.</strong> It draws what your workflow produced. It computes nothing, changes nothing and decides nothing \u2014 if a number looks wrong on the map, the step that produced it is where to look.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>A map is a stack of <strong>layers</strong>, drawn in order from the ground up:</p><p>1. a <strong>basemap</strong>, if you choose one \u2014 streets or terrain, under everything else 2. a <strong>study area</strong>, as the ground your results sit on 3. <strong>polygons</strong> \u2014 catchments, hulls, cells or bands 4. <strong>points</strong> \u2014 your records, sites, or anything with a location 5. a <strong>raster</strong> or a <strong>street network</strong>, where you have one</p><p>You connect what you want drawn. There is no setting that says what kind of map this is.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Connect</th><th>To draw</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a study area</td><td>the boundary your work is inside</td></tr><tr><td>a point layer</td><td>records with locations \u2014 up to eight separate layers</td></tr><tr><td>catchment polygons</td><td>areas reached, covered or grouped</td></tr><tr><td>a reasoning result</td><td>points coloured by the decision a ruleset reached</td></tr><tr><td>a coverage check</td><td>points marked inside, outside or missing a location</td></tr><tr><td>a raster</td><td>prepared or clipped cells</td></tr><tr><td>a street network</td><td>the streets themselves</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. <strong>Add the Map</strong> from the palette, under Outputs. 2. <strong>Connect your first layer.</strong> Drag from any step that produced something drawable onto the map's <strong>+ Add a layer</strong> port, or pick it from <strong>Add a layer</strong> in the Inspector. The map works out which port it belongs to. 3. <strong>Add more layers</strong> the same way. A second point source gets a layer of its own rather than replacing the first. 4. <strong>Choose how it is drawn.</strong> *Static plot* exports as an SVG with a scale bar and a north arrow. *Interactive map* can be panned and zoomed, and is the only one that can show a basemap. 5. <strong>Choose a basemap</strong> if the geography matters \u2014 streets or terrain under your results. Leaving it at *None* keeps the map working with no connection at all. 6. <strong>Title it</strong>, and write the source note. Both appear on the map and in its export. 7. <strong>Run the workflow.</strong> The map appears as a Results tab.</p><h4>Reading what you get</h4><ul><li>The <strong>key</strong> names every layer drawn.</li><li>The <strong>scale bar</strong> and <strong>north arrow</strong> appear on a static plot. An interactive map has no</li></ul><p>single scale, so it shows neither.</p><ul><li>The <strong>source note</strong> carries whatever you wrote, plus the licence of anything the map used.</li></ul><h4>Symbols and colour</h4><p>Two things about a point are shown at once, in two separate channels, so neither has to be read off the other.</p><ul><li><strong>Colour says which layer the point came from.</strong> Each point layer you connect gets its own hue</li></ul><p>from the categorical palette, so two searches of two different sources are told apart on the map and listed separately in the layer control, where either can be switched off.</p><ul><li><strong>Fill says where the point stands.</strong> Filled inside the study area, hollow outside it, grey when</li></ul><p>the record has no usable location \u2014 those are not drawn on the map and are listed instead.</p><ul><li><strong>Each layer also gets a mark</strong>: circle, square, triangle, star, cross or X. A mark is chosen per</li></ul><p>layer in this widget's settings, and the legend draws the same mark the map draws. Colour alone is not readable by every reader, and a figure printed in greyscale has no colour at all.</p><ul><li><strong>Every symbol carries a dark outline</strong>, so it reads over any basemap \u2014 a terrain basemap runs</li></ul><p>through the same colours the palette uses, and a hollow mark over one is hard to pick out. It is a neutral, not a seventh colour, and a checkbox turns it off for a pale ground or a printed figure.</p><ul><li><strong>Ordered values use an ordered ramp.</strong> Travel-time bands run light to dark through one hue,</li></ul><p>which is a ColorBrewer sequential scheme. A category palette on an ordered variable invites the reader to see difference where there is sequence.</p><p>The map's receipt records all of this \u2014 which channel carries which variable, the palette and the authority it comes from, and each layer's mark and colour with whether you chose it or the map derived it \u2014 so a figure in a publication can be attributed.</p><h3>Worked examples that use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Example</th><th>What the map shows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>John Snow \xB7 outbreak extent</strong></td><td>the judged outline over 250 death locations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>John Snow \xB7 Voronoi catchments</strong></td><td>one catchment per pump, with deaths overlaid</td></tr><tr><td><strong>John Snow \xB7 walking catchments</strong></td><td>travel-time bands, as a plot and as an interactive map</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Old Naledi \xB7 diagnostic access</strong></td><td>facilities coloured by the decision a ruleset reached</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Old Naledi \xB7 raster clipping</strong></td><td>a clipped raster inside its boundary</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Manila \xB7 coastal exposure</strong></td><td>facilities with their assigned projection site</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Geoprivacy</strong></td><td>the same records before and after being moved</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It cannot draw everything at once.</strong> A map with both a raster and polygons connected draws</li></ul><p>the raster. The layers stay connected; only one is drawn. A street network is no longer one of these cases: points, a study area and a street network wired to one map are all drawn together, on the interactive presentation.</p><ul><li><strong>The static plot does not draw a street network beside points.</strong> The streets are drawn in a</li></ul><p>frame of their own size, so a map carrying both draws the points in the plot and says where the streets are. Set the presentation to interactive to see all of it in one picture.</p><ul><li><strong>It cannot reorder layers, or choose their colours.</strong> Draw order is fixed, and a layer's hue</li></ul><p>comes from its position in the palette. The mark is the one part of the symbol you choose.</p><ul><li><strong>A basemap is not included in the SVG export.</strong> The tiles come from an outside service as</li></ul><p>you look at the map, so a figure you export shows your layers on a blank ground.</p><ul><li><strong>Choosing a basemap sends the area you are viewing to that service.</strong> Everything else about</li></ul><p>this map works offline. For maps of sensitive locations that is worth knowing before you switch it on.</p><ul><li><strong>It cannot tell you whether a map is honest.</strong> Emphasis, framing and colour all change how a</li></ul><p>map reads, and none of them changes a number. See the layers you connected, and say in the source note what the map is about.</p>",network_input:"<h2>Street network</h2><p><strong>An input widget.</strong> It supplies the streets that walking-distance work is measured along: a graph of points joined by segments, each with a length.</p><h3>Three ways in</h3><p>Choosing one shows that method's controls and hides the others, so the panel describes the path you are on.</p><p><strong>Upload a network file.</strong> A GraphML or JSON graph prepared elsewhere. It keeps its own extent.</p><p><strong>Download from OpenStreetMap.</strong> The study area's bounds go to overpass-api.de, which is asked to count what it would send before anything is sent, so an area past the limit is refused rather than transferred and discarded. One request at a time, 60 seconds minimum between them plus 30 seconds per megabyte received: the service is donated and the pause is the courtesy.</p><p><strong>Read an extract on this device.</strong> A published <code>.osm.pbf</code> \u2014 Geofabrik's country files, or any other OSM extract \u2014 chosen with the file picker. The file is read where it sits and is never copied into this browser's storage; the study area's bounding box is applied while the extract is decoded, so a country file yields one province's streets. Most countries have no province-level extract published, which is the point: the same file serves every province and every workflow after the first, and nothing is downloaded twice.</p><p>The travel mode belongs to all three. Walking keeps footways, paths and steps; driving keeps roads and honours vehicle one-ways. At the scale of a province the choice decides how much there is to read; at the scale of a district it decides what the question means. An acquired ADM1 or ADM0 study area defaults to driving, because nobody walks across a province.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>It gives the workflow a walkable street network, either downloaded from OpenStreetMap for your study area or uploaded from a file you prepared elsewhere.</p><p>A downloaded network keeps the ways people can walk on \u2014 residential streets, footways, paths, steps, tracks, service roads and the ordinary road classes \u2014 and leaves out anything tagged as closed to pedestrians. Each way becomes a run of segments between the points it was drawn with.</p><p><strong>Graph limit.</strong> The limit counts every point in the graph, including each bend a street was drawn with, not only the places two streets meet. A town can hold three points for every real intersection and a road following a coastline can hold seventeen, so the same limit allows very different amounts of street in different places. The download size allowed follows this number, so raising it raises what can be fetched.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><p>It takes an <code>area</code> \u2014 the study area that says where to download \u2014 and emits a <code>network</code>, which the isochrone, the junction reachability step, the corridor and the snapping step all accept. A map can draw one directly.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. Connect a <strong>Study area</strong>, then open the Street network node. 2. Leave <strong>Input method</strong> on *Download from OpenStreetMap*, or switch to upload if you have a file. 3. Press <strong>Check endpoint status</strong> if you want to know what the servers can do right now: it reports free slots and, when there are none, when the next one frees. 4. Press <strong>Download and save OSM network</strong>. This is a separate action \u2014 running the workflow does not start a download. 5. If the area is large or the streets dense, raise the graph limit and download again.</p><p>Downloads are paced: one at a time, with a pause afterwards that grows with the size of what you received. A busy server is waited for rather than treated as a failure. A network you have already downloaded is kept, so asking the same question again costs no request at all.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><p>It does not download while you run the workflow. Acquisition is always something you ask for.</p><p>An area too large for one request is asked for in several, automatically. The single request is tried first; if the service's own count says it will not fit, the panel says so \u2014 *&quot;Too much for one request \u2014 working out how many parts it takes&quot;* \u2014 and then works through the tiles, reporting each. The grid comes from that count, so a dense city is divided more finely than an empty district of the same size, and a sparse area is still one request. Tiles overlap by the download margin so a street crossing a seam keeps both ends, and parts of the grid that miss your study area are never requested at all. <strong>Stop</strong> ends the whole sequence, and the pause earned is charged once for the total rather than once per part.</p><p>Whatever route it took, the result is clipped to your <strong>study area polygon</strong>, not to the box around it. A junction outside the outline is not in the network unless it falls within your download margin, which is fetched on purpose so journeys near the border still work; a road is cut at the border rather than trailing beyond it. The widget records how many junctions and segments that removed.</p><p>It does not stop a download that is arriving. A request that never begins to answer is given three minutes, which is a limit on waiting, not on downloading. Once the first bytes arrive there is a download in progress and no clock ends it, however long it takes: press <strong>Stop</strong> when you want to give up, and the panel says how much had arrived and that none of it was kept. While it runs the panel shows how much has arrived and how fast, averaged over the last few seconds, so a transfer in trouble reads as a falling number rather than a frozen one. If it goes quiet for ninety seconds the panel says that too, with how much had arrived, and keeps waiting for you to decide.</p><p>It does not offer to hand-edit a large graph. The <strong>Edit bounded network JSON</strong> box is for the kind of network a person writes or fixes by hand \u2014 the Soho graph is about 28 KB. Above 20,000 nodes-plus-edges it is not offered, and the panel says so. Serialising a province into that box cost 41.8 MB of pretty-printed JSON and 59.7 MB again after escaping, rebuilt every time the panel redrew, which was enough to kill the browser tab while nothing was being done. The file input beside it stays, so a large graph can still be replaced by a small one, and nothing about the graph itself changes: it is held, drawn, run and exported at any size. The limit is declared as the cap <code>json-editor-elements</code>, queryable in the ontology like the rest.</p><p>It does not guess your area. Without a connected study area there is nothing to bound the request.</p><p>It downloads one travel mode at a time. Choose walking or driving before you download, because the two are different graphs and not two labels on one:</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Walking keeps</th><th>Driving keeps</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Footways, paths, steps, pedestrian ways</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td>Motorways, trunk roads and their slip roads</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>Ordinary streets \u2014 residential, unclassified, tertiary to primary</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>One-way restrictions it obeys</td><td>the ones that apply on foot</td><td>vehicle one-ways, and roundabouts</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A saved graph keeps the mode it was downloaded for, and the widget says which. To change mode, change it here and download again: the new graph replaces the old one. A widget further down the workflow that asks for a mode this graph cannot carry refuses the run and says so, rather than reporting minutes for a journey the streets do not support.</p><p>Distances along it are distances on those streets, and a network downloaded today describes the streets today, not the streets of whatever period your data comes from.</p><p>Before anything large is downloaded, the widget asks the service <strong>how much is there</strong>. Overpass answers that question directly, and an area holding more street data than this widget can take is refused with the number \u2014 after one cheap request rather than after several large ones that fail. An area so large that the service will not even count it is refused the same way, because that is what a public instance says when an area is beyond what it will process.</p><p>A saved network is kept beside the workflow rather than inside it, in the larger store your browser offers, so the size of a graph is no longer limited by what a workflow may hold. If that store is unavailable \u2014 private browsing, for instance \u2014 the application says &quot;small store only&quot; when it saves, and a large network may then be refused.</p><p>A driving graph carries no speed limits, no junction delay and no congestion. Travel time over it is one speed applied to every street, which is the same model walking uses and a weaker one for a car.</p><h3>Worked examples that use it</h3><p><strong>John Snow network isochrones</strong>, in the 1854 Cholera Outbreak in London project, uses a prepared Soho network to draw what each pump could be reached from on foot.</p>",point_input:"<h2>Point data</h2><p><strong>An input widget.</strong> It brings located records into a workflow \u2014 one row per thing, each with a position and whatever attributes came with it.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>It holds a set of points and the values attached to them: cases, households, facilities, pumps, sample sites. Everything downstream that works on points starts here.</p><p>Records arrive three ways. <strong>Import a file</strong> \u2014 GeoJSON, or a CSV with longitude and latitude columns. <strong>Place pins</strong> by clicking a map, for a handful of locations you know. <strong>Connect a source</strong> that produces points, such as a facility search.</p><p>A record may have no position. That is kept rather than dropped, because a case with no recorded address is still a case, and steps that need geometry say how many they had to leave out instead of quietly working with fewer.</p><p><strong>Columns and their kinds.</strong> Each attribute has a declared kind \u2014 number, text, or a term from a list you give. The data quality panel profiles a column, shows which values do not match its kind, and offers a repair. A repair is recorded with the node and appears in the run evidence; it never overwrites what the file held.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><p>It has no input unless you connect a source. Its output is <code>points</code>, which nearly everything accepts: maps, tables, distance measurement, catchments, summaries, rates, case series and the geoprivacy steps.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. Add the node and open it in the Inspector. 2. Import a file, or switch to pins and click the map. 3. Check the column kinds. A column read as text when it holds numbers will not band or sum. 4. Open <strong>Data quality</strong> to see what is missing or malformed before you build on it. 5. Give the node a label that says what the records are, not where they came from.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><p>It does not clean data silently. Nothing is coerced, dropped or filled in; what cannot be read is reported and left for you to decide.</p><p>It does not join one set of records to another. Two sets that share no key stay two sets.</p><p>It does not know what a row means. Whether 250 rows are 250 people or 250 addresses carrying 489 events is something your labels and your weights have to say.</p><h3>Worked examples that use it</h3><p><strong>John Snow Voronoi catchments</strong> loads 250 death locations and eight pumps. <strong>Measles in Hagelloch, 1861</strong> loads a 188-case line list. <strong>Old Naledi diagnostic access</strong> loads a facility registry.</p>",rate_ratio:"<h2>Rate ratio with an interval</h2><p><strong>A comparison.</strong> It takes a rate with several groups, divides one group's rate by another's, and reports the ratio with an interval and the counts behind it.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>A rate tells you how common something was in one group. It does not tell you whether that is more or less common than somewhere else, and a reader comparing two rates by eye is doing arithmetic without an interval.</p><p>John Snow's argument is the example. Houses supplied by the Southwark and Vauxhall Company had 1,263 cholera deaths among 40,046 houses; houses supplied by the Lambeth Company had 98 among 26,107. Those are two rates. The <strong>rate ratio is 8.40</strong>, with a 95% interval of <strong>6.84 to 10.32</strong>, and that sentence is the finding \u2014 not the two rates side by side.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><p><strong>rates</strong> \u2014 a *Rate with a denominator*, not a plain table. Only a rate declares what its numbers are per, and that declaration is what makes the division meaningful. A table of numbers connected here is refused by name.</p><p>The rate must have at least two groups, which means it needs a <strong>group by</strong> column.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it decides</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Exposed group</strong></td><td>Whose rate goes on top.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Compared to</strong></td><td>The reference group, on the bottom.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Interval level</strong></td><td>90%, 95% or 99%.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Interval method</strong></td><td>Log-normal, which is the only one implemented.</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>Which group goes on top</h4><p>This is a question about the sentence you intend, not about the data. A ratio of 8.40 and a ratio of 0.12 are the same finding written two ways, and only one of them is the sentence you meant. Put the group you are making a claim about on top, and the group you are comparing it with underneath.</p><h4>The interval level</h4><p>A 90% interval and a 95% interval are different claims, so the level is carried on the result rather than assumed. Widening the level widens the interval; it does not make the finding stronger.</p><h3>Reading what you get</h3><p>A one-row table carrying the ratio, and a receipt carrying everything behind it: both counts, both denominators, what the denominator counts, the bounds, the level and the method by name.</p><p><strong>There is no multiplier.</strong> A rate ratio is dimensionless \u2014 deaths per 1,000 divided by deaths per 1,000 cancels the per-1,000 \u2014 so &quot;8.40 per 10,000&quot; is not a quantity, and there is deliberately nowhere to write one. Change the rate's multiplier and the ratio does not move.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It will not divide by zero events.</strong> A group with no events has no ratio and no interval.</li></ul><p>Dulwich recorded 0 cholera deaths in Snow's seven weeks; the usual repair is to add half an event to every cell, which is a choice somebody made and not a fact about Dulwich. This refuses and says so.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not hide a small count.</strong> Putney's single death produces an interval spanning more than</li></ul><p>twentyfold. The ratio is reported with a warning naming the count, because the number is not wrong \u2014 it is just carrying almost no information, and only the count shows that.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not compare two different quantities.</strong> Both rates come from one rate node, so they</li></ul><p>share a denominator unit by construction. Deaths per house over deaths per person is not a ratio of anything.</p><ul><li><strong>It assumes the events are independent, and a transmitted disease is not.</strong> The interval comes</li></ul><p>from a Poisson model, where the variance of a log count is one over the count. Deaths that cluster \u2014 in a household, a street, around a pump \u2014 are over-dispersed relative to that, so the interval is <strong>narrower than the data warrants</strong>. The point estimate is unaffected, which is no comfort, because the interval is the claim. Expect the question and do not meet it for the first time in front of an audience.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not give you a p-value, and it is not a causal claim.</strong> An interval is a range</li></ul><p>compatible with the data. The ratio is a contrast between two populations as they were supplied, and nothing here adjusts for anything the two groups did not share.</p>",relate_table:"<h2>Relate a column from another table</h2><p><strong>A join, narrowly.</strong> It takes two tables that share a column, and brings a *category* from the second one onto the rows of the first.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>Two tables often describe the same subjects and carry different things about them. John Snow's Table VIII gives each of 32 South London sub-districts its population and its cholera deaths; his Table XII gives each of the same 32 sub-districts the company that supplied its water. Neither table alone can answer *how many people died in the sub-districts supplied by one company*, and until this widget existed the application could not put them together \u2014 so the example repository carried that answer as a table somebody had computed by hand.</p><p>Relating Table XII's <code>water_supply</code> onto Table VIII gives every row a supply category. Nothing is added up yet; that is the next step.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Port</th><th>What goes there</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>records</strong></td><td>the table being added to</td></tr><tr><td><strong>related</strong></td><td>the table being read from</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both are tables. The order matters: the first keeps its rows and gains a column, the second is only read.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it decides</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Matched on</strong></td><td>the column both tables carry, such as <code>sub_district</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bring across</strong></td><td>the second table's column to add, such as <code>water_supply</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h4>Why it brings a category and not a number</h4><p>A table in this application is <strong>long</strong>: it has key columns, which hold labels, and one value column, which holds numbers. A water supply company is a label, so it can only arrive as a key column \u2014 and a key column lands on every row, which is why <strong>the row count does not change.</strong></p><p>Bringing *numbers* across would mean adding rows instead, and that is a different operation with a different way of going wrong: two tables can disagree about the same measure. It is not built.</p><h3>Reading what you get</h3><p>The same table with one more column, and a receipt stating the matched column, the column brought across, how many rows were related, how many were dropped, and the distinct values that actually landed \u2014 which is what you need to see before grouping by it.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It will not choose between two answers.</strong> If the second table gives one key two different</li></ul><p>values of the column being brought across, this refuses and names the key. Taking the first would produce a grouping that is wrong in a way no total reveals, and the ambiguity is usually telling you the key does not identify what you think it identifies.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not invent a category.</strong> A key the second table has no row for is <strong>reported by name</strong></li></ul><p>and those rows are dropped. Filling in &quot;unknown&quot; would put a stratum in your table that nobody measured.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not match approximately.</strong> The match is on the exact string, after trimming. A</li></ul><p>sub-district spelled differently in two sources will not match, and this is Epi Info's <code>RELATE</code> rather than its <code>MATCH</code>, which is a different problem.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not tell you the two tables describe the same subjects.</strong> That is your judgement. The</li></ul><p>widget checks that the keys line up, not that they mean the same thing.</p><h3>Where it is used</h3><p>The South London example: *Died or survived, by water supply*.</p><h3>How it was checked</h3><p>Validation Lab check <strong>28</strong>, <code>28-relate-table-vs-pandas.ipynb</code>, against a pandas left merge of Snow's Table XII onto his Table VIII. There is no arithmetic to check, so what was recomputed is the matching: the same category on the same row, the row count unchanged at 64, and the same rows dropped and named when a sub-district is withheld.</p><p>The case worth an outside eye is the ambiguous key. A merge resolves it by <strong>multiplying rows</strong> \u2014 66 rows out of a 64-row table \u2014 so the sub-district's population would appear under both companies with no total revealing it. This refuses instead, and the check records the merge's row count as the measure of what the refusal prevents.</p><p>What it does not reach: Snow's two tables spell every sub-district identically, so a name differing by punctuation or case \u2014 the case you will actually meet \u2014 is never exercised.</p>",select_rows:"<h2>Select rows by value</h2><p><strong>A selection.</strong> It keeps the rows whose chosen column holds one of the values you name, in the order you name them, and drops the rest.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>What a figure should show is often less than what a table carries. After *Derive a value* computes <code>houses without a death</code>, Snow's Table IX holds three measures \u2014 <code>houses</code>, <code>deaths</code> and <code>houses without a death</code> \u2014 and only the last two belong in a 2\xD72, because <code>houses</code> is the <strong>total the other two partition</strong>. Leaving it in gives the grid a third column and a totals row that counts every house twice.</p><p>Keeping <code>deaths</code> and <code>houses without a death</code> leaves exactly the 2\xD72, and its totals come out as the houses supplied.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><p><strong>records</strong> \u2014 a table. It gives back the same table with fewer rows. Key columns, value field and unit are unchanged, because selecting rows does not change what a table measures.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it decides</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Key column to choose by</strong></td><td>Which column the values belong to.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Values to keep</strong></td><td>Ticked from the values on offer, or typed one per line.</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Order matters.</strong> The rows come out in the order you name the values, so a 2\xD72 reads the way you asked for rather than the way the file happened to be written. Unticking a value and ticking it again moves it to the end, which is how you reorder without a second control.</p><h3>What it refuses, and why</h3><p><strong>A value the table does not hold is reported, not ignored.</strong> Asking for a row that is not there is usually a typo, or a table upstream that changed. Quietly returning fewer rows than you asked for is how a figure loses a category nobody noticed.</p><p><strong>Keeping nothing the table holds is refused</strong> rather than handing back an empty table.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><p>It will not tell you whether what you kept is a fair selection. Dropping the categories that disagree with you looks exactly like dropping a double-counted total. Decide which you are doing, and say so where a reader can see it.</p><p>It will not add a value that is missing. Naming a value the table does not hold leaves it out and reports it; it does not create an empty category to hold a place in a figure.</p><p>It will not change what the rows mean. The unit, the value field and the key columns are the table's, and they come back as they went in.</p><h3>What this does not establish</h3><p>That a selection is an honest one. Keeping a subset of categories is exactly how a figure misleads, and this widget cannot tell a partition from a cherry-pick: it will drop the rows that disagree with you as readily as the ones that double-count. The evidence records which values were kept and how many rows went, so the choice is at least visible to a reader.</p>",snap_to_network:`<h2>Snap to network</h2><p><strong>A process widget.</strong> It moves each of your sites onto the nearest junction of a street network, so that steps which travel along streets have somewhere to start.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>A route has to begin on the street network. Your sites almost never sit exactly on a junction \u2014 a pump is in a yard, a clinic is set back from the road \u2014 so this widget finds the nearest junction to each one and puts it there.</p><p>It also records <strong>how far each site moved</strong>, which is the number to watch.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Connect</th><th>What it needs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>your sites</td><td>the points to move</td></tr><tr><td>a street network</td><td>from the <strong>Street network</strong> widget</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Out come the same sites, at junctions, each carrying the distance it travelled. Feed them to <strong>Reachable junctions</strong> or a walking-catchment step.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. <strong>Add it</strong> from the palette, under Catchments. 2. <strong>Connect your sites and a street network.</strong> 3. <strong>Set the snap limit</strong> \u2014 how far a site may be from the network before the result says so. 4. <strong>Run the workflow</strong>, then read the snap distances in the output or the Inspector.</p><h3>Worked examples that use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Example</th><th>What it does there</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>John Snow \xB7 walking catchments</strong></td><td>puts the eight pumps onto the Soho street network before travel times are computed</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It will not leave your data where it was.</strong> Every site comes out at a junction. The output</li></ul><p>is derived data, not observations any more, and the furthest any site moved is recorded in the run evidence.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not drop a site that is far from the network.</strong> A site past the snap limit is still</li></ul><p>moved to its nearest junction and is <strong>marked</strong>: its first leg crosses ground no street covers. The widget glows red on the canvas and the Inspector lists each one with its distance. The limit decides whether the result says so, not whether the site is in it.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not tell you the move was acceptable.</strong> If a site travelled 200 m to reach a</li></ul><p>junction, everything measured afterwards starts 200 m from where it should, and that error appears nowhere in the final numbers. Check the snap distance every time.</p><p>When sites are marked, you have three honest options: raise the limit, extend the street network where it stops short, or accept the list and say so in what you publish.</p><h3>Checking it yourself</h3><p>The lab's <a href="https://digital-twin-ph.github.io/validation-lab/lab/index.html?path=19-snap-vs-pyproj.ipynb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">snapping notebook</a> checks the junction choice against pyproj and measures each displacement against a five-minute walking budget \u2014 one of the eight example pumps was decided by 0.43 m.</p>`,study_area:"<h2>Study area</h2><p><strong>An input widget.</strong> It defines where your work takes place: one shape, kept on this device, that every later step can be scoped to.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>A study area holds one piece of geography and makes it available to the rest of the workflow. You can draw it, or take it from a published administrative boundary.</p><p><strong>Drawn.</strong> A bounding box, a polygon you click out, or a circle around a point. Quick, and it is yours \u2014 nothing outside the workflow knows what it means.</p><p><strong>From a boundary source.</strong> Chosen by country and level from geoBoundaries: ADM1 is usually a province or region, ADM2 a district or city, and what each level means differs by country. The level is checked before anything is downloaded, so you see how many units there are and how detailed they are before committing. You then pick the units you want, and the shape you get is the one the source published, vertex for vertex.</p><p>The shape is stored in longitude, latitude order (CRS84). Applying it writes N3 facts you can read in the <strong>N3 &amp; evidence</strong> tab.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><p>It has no input. Its output is an <code>area</code>, which the map, the buffer, the raster window, the facility search, the point grid, the coverage check and the area measurement all accept.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. Add the node and open it in the Inspector. 2. Press <strong>Choose study area</strong>. Pan to where you are working. 3. Either draw \u2014 <strong>Bounding box</strong>, <strong>Polygon</strong> or <strong>Point and radius</strong> \u2014 or open <strong>Use an administrative boundary</strong>, enter a country, pick a level, press <strong>Check this level</strong>, and choose the units you need. 4. Press <strong>Apply study area</strong>. 5. Give it a label that says what it is. The label travels into the receipts.</p><p>Changing the shape later keeps the old one: the Inspector lists every extent you have kept and restores any of them, which records a new extent carrying the old shape rather than overwriting anything.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><p>It does not simplify a boundary you took from a source. A published polygon is a claim about where a jurisdiction is, and thinning it would quietly change that claim.</p><p>It does not fetch data by itself. It says *where*; a source widget says *what*.</p><p>A drawn shape is not a jurisdiction. If your result needs to be about a named place, take the boundary from a source so the receipt can say whose line it is.</p><p>Several selected units stay several shapes. They are not merged into one outline and never become a bounding box around the group.</p><h3>Worked examples that use it</h3><p><strong>Old Naledi \xB7 diagnostic access</strong> uses a drawn settlement boundary. <strong>Old Naledi \xB7 raster clipping</strong> scopes a population raster to the same shape. The four <strong>1854 Cholera Outbreak in London</strong> workflows each use their own Soho extent, which is why three of them differ.</p>",summarize_combined_area:"<h2>Combined area</h2><p>Takes areas and gives back one area, or takes polygons and gives back one polygons result. It combines whatever kind it is given, and hands back the same kind.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p><strong>Given two study areas</strong> \u2014 say two adjacent provinces \u2014 it carries both outlines through as the parts of one study area. That area can then be given to everything a study area feeds: the street network, the facility sources, the clip, the buffer.</p><p><strong>Given a polygons result</strong> \u2014 catchments, hulls, buffers \u2014 it dissolves the ones that overlap or touch into single polygons and leaves separate ones separate, in a metric projection you choose.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><table><thead><tr><th>port</th><th>what it takes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>polygons</code></td><td>a polygons result, to dissolve</td></tr><tr><td><code>area</code> and <code>area2</code></td><td>two study areas, to combine</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Every port is always there. What the widget *yields* follows what you wire: connect areas and the output is an area; connect polygons and it is a polygons result. Nothing to set.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p><strong>To combine two study areas.</strong> Add the widget, then connect one study area to <code>area</code> and the other to <code>area2</code>. The output becomes an area the moment the first one is wired, so it can be given to a Street network, a facility source, a clip or a buffer exactly as a single study area would be. Name it if you want; unnamed, it takes the parts' names joined with a plus.</p><p><strong>To dissolve a polygons result.</strong> Connect it to <code>polygons</code> and set the metric zone and hemisphere for the projection the dissolve is computed in. Overlapping and touching polygons become one; separate ones stay separate, and the count of each is reported.</p><h3>What it assumes</h3><p><strong>That a touching border should not be dissolved when combining areas.</strong> Two provinces that share a border come through as two parts, not one welded outline. A published outline is an authority's record of where a boundary lies; two authorities describe a shared border differently, and welding them would assert a third line that neither drew while still citing both as the source. Every question this application asks of a study area \u2014 is this point inside, what does an extract clip to, what are the bounds \u2014 is answered identically by two parts. What they do not do is pretend the border is gone.</p><p><strong>That dissolving IS right for polygons.</strong> A catchment is this application's own construction, not somebody's record of where a line is, so two that overlap are one reachable region and are merged.</p><p><strong>That the first area's spatial reference is the combination's.</strong> Combining areas held in different projections is a question nobody has asked here yet, and guessing an answer would bury it.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li>It will not dissolve areas, for the reason above.</li><li>It will not combine an area with a polygons result. They are different kinds and the output</li></ul><p>would have to be one of them.</p><ul><li>It will not reproject. The metric projection on the polygons path is yours to set; the area path</li></ul><p>carries the first part's reference through unchanged.</p><h3>What the receipt says</h3><p>On the area path: <code>fw:AreaCombination</code> as the activity, <code>prov:used</code> for each part, <code>fw:partCount</code>, and <code>fw:ringCount</code> on the geometry \u2014 so a reader can see the parts were kept rather than merged.</p><h3>Checked against</h3><p>The polygons path is checked against shapely/GEOS in the validation lab (<code>12-combined-area</code>, agrees: combined area 701,662.61 m\xB2 against 701,662.61 m\xB2, zero symmetric difference). <strong>That check covers the polygons path only.</strong> The area path, added 2026-10-22, has no external check yet, and no Dive Deeper lesson \u2014 those are two different repositories and two different questions: the lab recomputes what this widget computed, and a lesson teaches what the method IS.</p>",summarize_hull:`<h2>Observed extent (convex hull)</h2><p><strong>A process widget.</strong> It takes points and gives back one polygon: the smallest convex outline that contains them, with the area it encloses.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>Stretch an elastic band around all your points and let it go. The shape it settles into is the convex hull \u2014 the outline of where your observations are, with no dents.</p><p>Along with the shape you get two measurements the rest of your workflow can read: the <strong>area</strong> it encloses, in square metres, and its <strong>span</strong>, the distance across its longest axis.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Connect</th><th>What it needs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a point layer</td><td>at least three points that have locations</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Out comes one polygon. Connect it to a Map to see it, a Table to read its numbers, or a Ruleset to make a judgement about its size.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. <strong>Add it</strong> from the palette, under Summaries. 2. <strong>Connect a point layer.</strong> Points without a location sit the step out, and the notes say how many took part. 3. <strong>Check the zone.</strong> The widget measures in metres, so it projects your points first, and the UTM zone it uses is part of the answer. The Inspector suggests the zone that fits your data and tells you what a different one would cost. 4. <strong>Run the workflow</strong>, then read the area on the output or draw the outline on a map.</p><h3>Worked examples that use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Example</th><th>What it does there</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>John Snow \xB7 outbreak extent</strong></td><td>outlines the 250 death locations, and a ruleset judges whether the outbreak extent is large enough to look at</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It will not follow the shape of your data.</strong> A hull is convex, and cases along a river or</li></ul><p>facilities along a coast are not. The outline fills in the space between them, so it claims ground where nothing was observed. If that matters, do not use a hull.</p><ul><li><strong>It is not a boundary.</strong> It is an outline of what was *seen* \u2014 not a service area, not a</li></ul><p>catchment, not anything anyone agreed to. Where your observation stopped is not where the thing you were observing stopped.</p><ul><li><strong>Three points in a line enclose nothing</strong>, and the widget refuses rather than returning an</li></ul><p>area of zero.</p><ul><li><strong>Watch the span against the area.</strong> A hull much longer than it is wide is close to a line</li></ul><p>and should be read as one. The widget says so in its notes when it happens.</p><h3>Checking it yourself</h3><p>The lab's <a href="https://digital-twin-ph.github.io/validation-lab/lab/index.html?path=10-hull-vs-shapely.ipynb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">hull notebook</a> recomputes the outline and the area with Shapely and compares them to this widget's.</p>`,table_output:"<h2>Table</h2><h3>What it does</h3><p>Shows what you connect to it, as a table in its own Results tab.</p><p>It takes four different things, and it works out which it is showing from what you wire:</p><ul><li>a <strong>keyed table</strong> from any step that produces one \u2014 Relate a column, Total within groups, a rate,</li></ul><p>a two-by-two, an imported table;</p><ul><li><strong>point data</strong>, with coordinates and attributes;</li><li><strong>catchment polygons</strong> and their summaries;</li><li><strong>reasoning results</strong>, with their decisions.</li></ul><h3>What you connect</h3><p>Every port is on the node from the moment you add it. There is nothing to choose first.</p><p>Connect a step's output to the matching port and the table shows it. If you wire more than one, the more specific wins: a keyed table before polygons, polygons before decisions, and the point path is the general one.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. Add it, or use <strong>Quick Look</strong> on a step and open its table from there. 2. Drag from the step's output to the port that matches what it produces. 3. Name the Results tab.</p><p>Nothing is required. A table with nothing connected is <strong>unfinished</strong>, not broken, and says so.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It will not ask you what shape your data is.</strong> There is no mode to set, and connecting</li></ul><p>something never removes a connection you already made. <strong>Map</strong> works the same way.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not chart the table.</strong> Charting one means choosing which key varies and whether to</li></ul><p>aggregate, which is a decision rather than a port. Use <strong>Chart</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not total anything.</strong> It shows the rows it is given. Use <strong>Total within groups</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Where it is used</h3><p>The South London example, five times: the mortality rate table, both water-company tables and both contingency tables.</p><h3>How it was checked</h3><p>Unit tests over the port list, the renderer the connections imply, and the fallback to a stored mode for a workflow saved before the mode was removed. One pins the defect directly: a freshly added table offers a <code>table</code> port with no mode set.</p><p>What no test establishes is whether the four renderers should be one widget at all. The split is inherited and is recorded as open in [experiment 21](../experiments/21-shared-result-outputs.md).</p>",two_by_two:"<h2>Two-by-two table</h2><p><strong>The analysis under the table.</strong> It takes four counts \u2014 two groups by two outcomes \u2014 and reports the risk in each group, the risk ratio, the odds ratio and the risk difference, each naming the method that produced it.</p><h3>What it does</h3><p>A two-by-two is the central table of field epidemiology, and this application could draw one for months before it could analyse one: it showed the four counts and their margins and stopped there.</p><p>On Snow's South London people, built inside the application from two of his tables:</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>died</th><th>survived</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Southwark and Vauxhall only</strong></td><td>844</td><td>166,810</td><td>167,654</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lambeth only</strong></td><td>18</td><td>19,115</td><td>19,133</td></tr></tbody></table><table><thead><tr><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Risk, exposed</td><td>5.03 per 1,000</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Risk, unexposed</td><td>0.94 per 1,000</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Risk ratio</strong></td><td><strong>5.35</strong></td><td>3.36 to 8.53 \xB7 Katz log</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Odds ratio</strong></td><td><strong>5.37</strong></td><td>3.37 to 8.57 \xB7 Wald log</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Risk difference</strong></td><td><strong>4.09 per 1,000</strong></td><td>Wald unpooled</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Why this exists, and the number to be careful about</h3><p>The same project carries a <strong>rate ratio of 8.40</strong> on Snow's houses. The risk ratio here, on his people, is <strong>5.35</strong>. Both are correct and they are 57% apart.</p><p>It is tempting to say that is because one is a rate ratio and the other a risk ratio. <strong>It is not.</strong> A risk ratio over the same houses is <strong>8.401853429780243</strong> \u2014 the rate ratio's value exactly, bit for bit. Deaths per house *is* a risk when a house can hold at most one death, so the two are the same division. Only the intervals differ, and only in the second decimal: <strong>6.84 to 10.32</strong> from the rate ratio against <strong>6.85 to 10.31</strong> here.</p><p>So what moves the number from 8.40 to 5.35 is the <strong>subject being counted</strong>: a house, or a person. And that is the one thing the familiar layout does not show. A reader who takes 8.40 for the risk ratio beside the houses table is right \u2014 and is thereby taught that the substitution is always safe, which it is not. Make the outcome common instead of rare and the two part company.</p><p>This widget supplies the estimate. <strong>Reading the denominator is still yours.</strong></p><h3>What you connect</h3><p><strong>records</strong> \u2014 a table holding <strong>one row per combination</strong> of the exposure and the outcome. Total it first with *Total within groups*: a cell matching more than one row is refused rather than added up here, because summing silently is how a two-by-two acquires subjects nobody counted.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it decides</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Exposure column</strong></td><td>the column holding the groups</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Exposed</strong> / <strong>Unexposed</strong></td><td>which two of its values are being compared</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Outcome column</strong></td><td>the column holding the outcome</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Case</strong> / <strong>Non-case</strong></td><td>which value is the case</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Interval level</strong></td><td>90%, 95% or 99%</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>Why you name the two groups</h4><p>Because a table may hold more than two. Snow's people-denominated table has <strong>three</strong> supply categories: 16 of his 32 sub-districts were supplied by *both* companies. That stratum is not an exposure group, and it is the reason Snow's sub-district comparison could not settle the question and he went to the house level.</p><p>Whatever you leave out is <strong>named on the result</strong>, and the totals are of the two groups you chose rather than of the table. A stratum silently excluded is the commonest way a two-by-two misleads.</p><h3>Reading what you get</h3><p>The four cells as a table, with the estimates carried on it, and a receipt holding each estimate's value, bounds, level and <strong>method by name</strong>.</p><p><strong>Each estimate also carries the label the familiar layout prints for the same quantity</strong>, so you can find the row you were trained to look for:</p><table><thead><tr><th>this widget computes</th><th>printed as</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>risk ratio \xB7 Katz log</td><td><code>Risk Ratio (RR)</code></td></tr><tr><td>odds ratio \xB7 Wald log</td><td><code>Odds Ratio (cross product)</code></td></tr><tr><td>risk difference \xB7 Wald unpooled</td><td><code>Risk Difference (RD%)</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Read from Epi Info AI's results panel, not from Epi Info.</strong> That is a sibling project whose epi kernel was synthesized from the original, so these are a good deduction and not a verification \u2014 and every estimate records which, in <code>epiInfoLabelSource</code>. The receipt says <code>deduced</code>. If somebody reads the labels off Epi Info itself the source becomes <code>verified</code>, and the words may or may not change; that is a different claim and the field keeps the two apart.</p><p><code>Odds Ratio (cross product)</code> keeps its qualifier on purpose: the same panel prints an <code>Odds Ratio (conditional MLE)</code> beside it, which is a different estimator and is <strong>not</strong> computed here. Dropping the qualifier would make this label claim both.</p><p><strong>One scale difference, stated rather than reconciled.</strong> That panel prints the risk difference as a *percentage*. Snow's figure is <code>0.004093</code> here and <code>0.4093%</code> there \u2014 the same quantity on a different scale, and the estimate says so where the number is, so nobody reads the two screens side by side and concludes the implementations disagree.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It will not claim agreement with Epi Info.</strong> These are standard methods, named honestly, with</li></ul><p>labels deduced from a project built from Epi Info. A parity claim needs a parity suite nobody has written.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not treat an empty cell as a zero.</strong> An empty cell is unknown, and a two-by-two</li></ul><p>computed as though it were zero would understate that group.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not add half a case to make a ratio computable.</strong> A group with no cases has no risk</li></ul><p>ratio, and this says so. The usual repair is an assumption, not a measurement.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not withhold the estimates it can still compute.</strong> A zero cell removes the odds ratio</li></ul><p>and leaves the risk difference perfectly well defined, so each estimate reports its own availability and its own reason.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not accept a table of rates or percentages.</strong> These estimates count subjects. A</li></ul><p>non-whole number is refused.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not adjust for anything.</strong> This is a contrast between two groups as they were supplied.</li></ul><p>The stratum you left out is excluded, not controlled for, and no estimate here is an effect.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not test a hypothesis.</strong> Chi-square, Mantel-Haenszel, Yates, Fisher exact and mid-P are</li></ul><p>not built. They are a larger piece and were not started to meet a date.</p><h3>Where it is used</h3><p>The South London example: *Died or survived, by water supply*.</p>",map_image_input:"<h2>Georeferenced map image</h2><h3>What it does</h3><p>Draws a scanned or published map <strong>underneath</strong> your workflow, in your workflow's own projection, so a modern result sits over historical ground.</p><p>It was built for John Snow's 1855 Map 2, whose blue, red and purple washes show which company supplied which part of south London \u2014 and whose purple intermingled zone is the whole point of his design. Put the sub-district boundaries over it and a reader can see for themselves whether the two agree.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><p>Nothing goes in. It is a source: you give it an image and a georeference.</p><p>Its output goes to a <strong>Map</strong>, on the <code>ground</code> port.</p><h3>How to use it</h3><p>1. Add it and choose the image \u2014 a JPEG or PNG of a map. 2. Give it the georeference, either as a world file (<code>.jgw</code>, <code>.pgw</code>, <code>.wld</code>) or as four corners in degrees: west, south, east, north. 3. Connect it to a Map, and set the opacity so you can read what is drawn on top.</p><h3>Reading what you get</h3><p>The figure says which plate it drew, where it came from, and <strong>about how many metres one of its pixels covers</strong>. Snow's plate over south London is about 16 metres per pixel: enough to recognise the river and the parish outlines, not enough to read a street.</p><p>The receipt records the image's digest, its pixel size, the four bounds, that ground resolution, and <code>layerRole &quot;context&quot;</code>.</p><h3>What it will not do</h3><ul><li><strong>It will not let you measure it.</strong> The output carries its own port type, so it cannot be</li></ul><p>connected to Clip raster or Sample raster at all. A plate's pixels are a printer's ink: sampling them returns the colour of a line, which is a number with no referent. This is the one thing the widget refuses by construction rather than by warning.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not join anything to what the map shows.</strong> A boundary drawn on an 1855 plate is a</li></ul><p>picture of a boundary. Use a study area for the frame.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not draw a rotated plate.</strong> If the world file carries rotation terms, it stops and says</li></ul><p>so. Drawing a rotated image as an upright rectangle would put the map in the wrong place while looking entirely correct.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not reproject.</strong> The corners are read as CRS84 degrees. Corners outside the world</li></ul><p>usually mean a projected georeference, and it refuses rather than reading one datum's coordinates as another's \u2014 an error that moves a map by hundreds of metres and still looks like a map.</p><ul><li><strong>It will not tell you the georeference is right.</strong> It draws where you said. An alignment error</li></ul><p>looks exactly like a correct placement.</p><h3>Where it is used</h3><p>The South London example: Snow's Map 2 under the sub-district choropleth.</p><h3>How it was checked</h3><p>Unit tests over the world-file reader, the bounds it derives from it, the ground resolution, and each refusal \u2014 a rotated world file, a flipped image, crossed corners and corners outside the world. A browser test places a plate under a figure and confirms it is drawn beneath the workflow's own layers rather than over them.</p><p>What no test establishes is whether the georeference is <strong>correct</strong>. See [experiment 143](../experiments/143-a-georeferenced-plate-as-ground.md), which records that measuring the residual at the plate's four control points against modern boundaries is what would close it, and that it has not been done.</p>"};var i=r,l=e=>Object.hasOwn(i,e),p=e=>`https://github.com/digital-twin-ph/fieldwork/blob/main/docs/widgets/${e}.md`;function g(e,o){return l(e)?`<button class="text-button" id="open-manual" data-manual="${o(e)}">Manual for this widget</button>`:""}function w(e,o,a){let s=i[e];if(!s)return;let t=document.createElement("dialog");t.id="manual-dialog",t.setAttribute("aria-labelledby","manual-title"),t.innerHTML=`<div class="manual-head"><h2 id="manual-title">${a(o)}</h2><button class="button" id="manual-close">Close</button></div><article class="manual-body">${s}</article><p class="muted">This page is part of the manual carried in this build. Its source is <a href="${p(e)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">docs/widgets/${a(e)}.md</a>, and the widget's ontology entry points at it with <code>fw:userManual</code>.</p>`,document.body.append(t);let n=()=>{t.close(),t.remove()};t.querySelector("#manual-close").onclick=n,t.addEventListener("cancel",h=>{h.preventDefault(),n()}),t.showModal()}export{i as a,l as b,p as c,g as d,w as e};
